1
00:00:16,027 --> 00:00:27,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is on Vipalasa Sutta, or also translated as the Inversions.

2
00:00:31,551 --> 00:00:39,673
[SPEAKER_00]: It's Anguttara Nikaya Book of Fours, number 49, because there are four inversions of perceptions.

3
00:00:40,893 --> 00:00:43,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Inversions of mind, inversions of view.

4
00:00:43,574 --> 00:00:44,174
[SPEAKER_00]: What four?

5
00:00:45,468 --> 00:00:52,793
[SPEAKER_00]: The inversion of perception, mind and view that takes impermanent to be permanent.

6
00:00:52,833 --> 00:00:53,053
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

7
00:00:53,113 --> 00:00:53,313
[SPEAKER_00]: 2.

8
00:00:53,653 --> 00:01:00,017
[SPEAKER_00]: The inversion of perception, mind and view that takes what is suffering to be pleasurable.

9
00:01:00,037 --> 00:01:00,378
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

10
00:01:00,458 --> 00:01:00,598
[SPEAKER_00]: 3.

11
00:01:00,938 --> 00:01:04,801
[SPEAKER_00]: The inversion of perception, mind and view that takes what is not self to be self.

12
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:06,482
[SPEAKER_00]: And No.

13
00:01:06,502 --> 00:01:06,602
[SPEAKER_00]: 4.

14
00:01:06,962 --> 00:01:11,065
[SPEAKER_00]: The inversion of perception, mind and view that takes what is unattractive to be attractive.

15
00:01:11,445 --> 00:01:14,647
[SPEAKER_00]: These are the four inversions of perception, mind and view.

16
00:01:16,548 --> 00:01:18,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, there are four non-inversions.

17
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:31,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-inversion that takes impermanent as impermanent, suffering as suffering, not-self as not-self, and unattractive as unattractive.

18
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:41,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first inversion of perception, mind and view is the inversion of impermanent as permanent.

19
00:01:41,849 --> 00:01:45,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, inversion of taking anicca to be nicca.

20
00:01:46,588 --> 00:01:55,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as we spoke in some previous talks, anicca, you can translate it as impermanence.

21
00:01:55,951 --> 00:01:56,671
[SPEAKER_00]: It's absolutely fine.

22
00:01:57,252 --> 00:02:12,236
[SPEAKER_00]: But it also needs to be understood in a sense... the impermanence needs to be understood in a sense that it's not like an impersonal observation of everything is constantly changing.

23
00:02:12,256 --> 00:02:13,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, nothing is really permanent.

24
00:02:14,362 --> 00:02:19,505
[SPEAKER_00]: The impermanence refers to the to the sense of self that is attached to the ownership of things.

25
00:02:20,306 --> 00:02:29,792
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you feel... Well, that's when you regard things impermanently, which is when when those things change, that's when you feel that impermanence as unpleasant.

26
00:02:30,433 --> 00:02:33,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, anicca and dukkha are the characteristics of things.

27
00:02:34,055 --> 00:02:39,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, impermanence, anicca, should be should be understood in the sense of the...

28
00:02:40,119 --> 00:02:43,341
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, things are impermanent in a sense of their changing,

29
00:02:43,907 --> 00:02:56,499
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's more like they're changing outside of the scope of your own will, of your own control, which means things can be pleasing, agreeable, can obey your will, until they don't.

30
00:02:56,759 --> 00:03:03,686
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a recognition that this outer, like what is inconceivable,

31
00:03:04,349 --> 00:03:08,670
[SPEAKER_00]: To access with your intention, with your will, with your control, that's where things are rooted in.

32
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:12,912
[SPEAKER_00]: They're rooted in that inaccessible, in that which you cannot control.

33
00:03:12,992 --> 00:03:14,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, they're impermanent.

34
00:03:14,872 --> 00:03:23,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, when things change, and they will, you won't be able to foresee it, so to speak, even if you expect.

35
00:03:24,155 --> 00:03:26,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, you expect something will change this very second.

36
00:03:27,776 --> 00:03:31,197
[SPEAKER_00]: When change happens, it's always a surprise on that existential level.

37
00:03:31,297 --> 00:03:33,478
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot see the moment that things change.

38
00:03:36,125 --> 00:03:38,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially if you're personally involved with it.

39
00:03:39,388 --> 00:03:43,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so that's why, that's why anicca needs to be understood in that personal sense.

40
00:03:44,072 --> 00:03:49,096
[SPEAKER_00]: As Nyanavira also pointed out, it's not like that things that don't matter to us are impermanent.

41
00:03:49,216 --> 00:03:51,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, they're impermanent, but nobody cares about it.

42
00:03:51,678 --> 00:03:57,523
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha's teaching, the dhamma, deals with things that matter to you, to see them as impermanent.

43
00:03:58,104 --> 00:04:03,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, that's the, that's the practice and obviously that the liberation can result from that.

44
00:04:04,427 --> 00:04:12,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the, like, things that you're not involved with, emotionally, with a view, you don't really care.

45
00:04:12,949 --> 00:04:16,210
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, they can change and anything can happen to them will make a difference to you.

46
00:04:16,710 --> 00:04:23,192
[SPEAKER_00]: And that impermanence is not really impermanence that results in an anatta, if it's understood, because you don't really...

47
00:04:23,212 --> 00:04:28,433
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to take things that your sense of self is involved with.

48
00:04:28,453 --> 00:04:33,975
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a sense of self-view, there's the appropriation as mine, and then recognizing those things.

49
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:48,598
[SPEAKER_00]: While you're holding them as mine, as being outside of the scope, or even the possibility of your own intention, which means, fundamentally, they're utterly inaccessible.

50
00:04:49,099 --> 00:04:50,299
[SPEAKER_00]: They have arisen on their own.

51
00:04:50,739 --> 00:04:53,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, they will cease on their own.

52
00:04:54,841 --> 00:05:02,544
[SPEAKER_00]: You, your entire domain of control, sense of ownership, everything else, is completely secondary to it, and can never be more than that.

53
00:05:03,753 --> 00:05:12,115
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why Arahant, when he fully understands this, he doesn't... he doesn't turn into a robot or somebody who is not aware of his intentions.

54
00:05:12,135 --> 00:05:22,917
[SPEAKER_00]: He's just... he cannot anymore misconceive his intentions as to spill, so to speak, into this inaccessible domain of things' origination.

55
00:05:24,057 --> 00:05:31,419
[SPEAKER_00]: He recognizes the limit, the extent of possibility of control, intentional intention, cetanā and all.

56
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:34,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Having fully understood it, he stops misconceiving it.

57
00:05:35,412 --> 00:05:45,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, he can still intend, think, speak and so on, have preferences even, but none of that can pervert into that view of ownership.

58
00:05:46,317 --> 00:05:52,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how we come to these inversions or perversions of perception and view and the mind.

59
00:05:54,282 --> 00:05:58,844
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, everybody starts with a self-centered experience, with a sense of self.

60
00:05:58,964 --> 00:06:00,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Perversions of perception and the mind.

61
00:06:01,750 --> 00:06:10,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Every experience, unenlightened experience, starts with self-centeredness, with appropriation, which means that's what takes priority.

62
00:06:11,275 --> 00:06:12,896
[SPEAKER_00]: I am, thus things are mine.

63
00:06:14,476 --> 00:06:26,922
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person starts to practice and ponder on on anicca correctly, then it... well, then the evidence is going to start to contradict the assumed picture of your own experience, of that sense of self.

64
00:06:27,643 --> 00:06:29,103
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you start to realize that

65
00:06:31,287 --> 00:06:34,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Things are mine, not because I am.

66
00:06:34,669 --> 00:06:38,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It's I am, because I keep taking things as mine, mistakenly.

67
00:06:39,893 --> 00:06:47,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already... that's already that perversion of ownership that... that arises on account of the perversion of perception.

68
00:06:47,339 --> 00:06:58,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceiving things that have a reason on their own, outside of the scope of your own intention, of your own perception for that matter, perceiving them as if they are reason for you or within you.

69
00:07:00,025 --> 00:07:14,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you're already perverting that inherent impossibility of ownership, which is what the nature is, as something that you thoroughly own.

70
00:07:15,635 --> 00:07:24,601
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only... even that perversion is possible to exist only because things have arisen on their own, outside of your scope of your intention and perception.

71
00:07:26,398 --> 00:07:30,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why once this becomes apparent, well, then it's impossible.

72
00:07:31,241 --> 00:07:34,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you don't have to go and undo these perversions.

73
00:07:34,624 --> 00:07:37,365
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not knowing this, that's why it's perverted.

74
00:07:38,866 --> 00:07:46,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even that perversion cannot escape the nature of things having arisen outside of the scope of your ownership.

75
00:07:48,493 --> 00:07:50,434
[SPEAKER_00]: The nature of things as impermanent.

76
00:07:51,395 --> 00:07:54,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you pervert them or not, they are impermanent.

77
00:07:54,838 --> 00:07:58,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Perversion just keeps you blind in regard to that impermanence.

78
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, when impermanence is seen correctly, the perversion is gone.

79
00:08:03,803 --> 00:08:04,264
[SPEAKER_00]: And the same.

80
00:08:04,304 --> 00:08:09,747
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you perceive, that's what... that's... those are the views you're going to be developing.

81
00:08:09,807 --> 00:08:13,449
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once the mind... once the perception is perverted, the views are perverted.

82
00:08:13,969 --> 00:08:15,410
[SPEAKER_00]: The assumption, the upādāna.

83
00:08:16,971 --> 00:08:21,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Once the views are perverted, then the whole experience, the whole mind is perverted, equally perverted.

84
00:08:21,534 --> 00:08:23,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... it's... it's not necessarily

85
00:08:24,528 --> 00:08:34,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Happening in succession, as to one, two, three, it's more like three layers of perversion, of simultaneously present perversion, that's all rooted in the same thing.

86
00:08:35,738 --> 00:08:50,271
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can undo, or you can understand the principle of perversion, and thus abandon it, through either understanding the perversion of impermanence, understanding the perversion of

87
00:08:52,186 --> 00:08:56,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Self and not-self and understanding the perversion of unattractive and attractive.

88
00:08:57,070 --> 00:08:59,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Because these three things share the same principle.

89
00:09:00,753 --> 00:09:04,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, we perceive attractive always on the basis of unattractive.

90
00:09:04,596 --> 00:09:05,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

91
00:09:05,117 --> 00:09:05,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Entirely.

92
00:09:05,977 --> 00:09:07,579
[SPEAKER_00]: There is absolutely no exception.

93
00:09:07,619 --> 00:09:08,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Like what I just said.

94
00:09:10,181 --> 00:09:12,543
[SPEAKER_00]: The perversion of perception is as permanent.

95
00:09:12,663 --> 00:09:17,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always on account of things having a reason on their own beforehand.

96
00:09:17,527 --> 00:09:21,450
[SPEAKER_00]: And that means a reason outside of the scope of your ownership or the possibility of your ownership.

97
00:09:22,713 --> 00:09:33,158
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even the the the factual attractive that we perceive in the world, it's within the scope of unattractive, which means it's not truly the attractive.

98
00:09:33,338 --> 00:09:39,601
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like less unattractive, but equally unattractive in terms of sharing the same nature.

99
00:09:40,621 --> 00:09:41,902
[SPEAKER_00]: And the sense of self, the same.

100
00:09:41,922 --> 00:09:44,803
[SPEAKER_00]: There comes what I mentioned with the sense of mind.

101
00:09:46,124 --> 00:09:51,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start undoing the sense of mind, then you realize that this self, which is

102
00:09:52,167 --> 00:09:54,708
[SPEAKER_00]: which has been taken to be me, to be I am.

103
00:09:55,429 --> 00:09:56,969
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually not in my control.

104
00:09:57,049 --> 00:09:57,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

105
00:09:57,330 --> 00:10:10,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's fully rooted, fully founded, fully arisen upon things that are outside of the scope of the... outside of the possibility, even, of the scope of my control, my conceiving, my... my understanding.

106
00:10:10,216 --> 00:10:18,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that... you discern the presence of the outside domain of impermanence without misconceiving it.

107
00:10:19,733 --> 00:10:22,135
[SPEAKER_00]: As the inside domain, as my domain, as for me.

108
00:10:23,236 --> 00:10:36,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, that which is self becomes not myself, because you cannot not see that that sense of self is fully embedded, fully enclosed within this utter fundamental sense of impermanence.

109
00:10:38,368 --> 00:10:38,988
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's like...

110
00:10:39,829 --> 00:10:43,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the possibility of escape, as in having something permanent apart from this,

111
00:10:44,577 --> 00:10:46,318
[SPEAKER_00]: It's thoroughly inconceivable.

112
00:10:46,659 --> 00:10:50,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in the wildest theory and imagination, you can't even conceive it as such.

113
00:10:51,062 --> 00:11:08,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And for as long as that's not very clear, that's how you keep conceiving it, and giving it being in this experience of your control, which is why you keep feeding the sense of that self-centeredness, that sense of self, the views, the perversion of perception, and so on.

114
00:11:09,696 --> 00:11:13,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me just... to finish this, eh...

115
00:11:18,354 --> 00:11:21,335
[SPEAKER_00]: The verse, so the verse from the same sutra, the verses, so...

116
00:11:21,695 --> 00:11:37,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Perceiving permanence in the impermanent, perceiving pleasure in what is suffering, perceiving a self in what is not self, perceiving attractiveness in what is unattractive, being resort to wrong views, their minds deranged, their perception twisted.

117
00:11:40,362 --> 00:11:46,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Such people are bound by the yoke of Mara and do not teach, do not reach security from bondage.

118
00:11:46,658 --> 00:11:49,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Beings continue in saṃsāra, going to birth and death.

119
00:11:50,259 --> 00:11:57,261
[SPEAKER_00]: But when Buddhas arise in the world, sending forth a brilliant light, they reveal this dhamma that leads to the stilling of suffering.

120
00:11:58,082 --> 00:12:01,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Having heard it, wise people have regained their sanity.

121
00:12:02,583 --> 00:12:08,185
[SPEAKER_00]: They have seen the impermanent as impermanent, and they've seen suffering as suffering.

122
00:12:08,745 --> 00:12:14,847
[SPEAKER_00]: They've seen what is not-self as not-self, and they've seen the unattractive as unattractive.

123
00:12:15,672 --> 00:12:19,697
[SPEAKER_00]: By the acquisition of the right view, they've overcome all suffering.

